TRAP HIT WHILE WRITING THIS TEST — worth recording, because the first version was
WRONG and passed against the regression above.

The first draft fed the DoFn an unparseable junk line. That looked fine (the stub was
reached on clean code, and the test passed) but was useless: an inlined duplicate throws
on junk input, hits its `except` fallback, calls route_intake anyway, and the stub's
sentinel appears regardless. The test asserted a property it could not actually detect.

Fix: drive the DoFn with a VALID, parseable record taken from the seeded harness. An
inlined duplicate then handles it entirely on its own and never reaches any fallback,
so the sentinel is absent and the assertion fires.

This is exactly the "passes for the wrong reason" failure mode that made the original
sabotage-and-revert check worth doing. A test asserting "these two things are wired
together" must be verified against a realistic break, not just observed to pass.
